  "command": "cat > /app/paper.txt << 'EOF'\n# Research Advances in Code Agents and Red-teaming\n\n## Abstract\n\nThe rapid advancement of code agents powered by large language models (LLMs) has transformed software development workflows, enabling automated code generation, execution, and debugging capabilities. However, these powerful autonomous systems introduce significant security and safety concerns, particularly around the execution of potentially harmful code. This paper provides a comprehensive survey of recent advances in code agent research, with a focus on red-teaming methodologies for evaluating and mitigating risks. We examine the intersection of code generation, code execution safety, and adversarial testing, highlighting emerging benchmarks and evaluation frameworks. Our analysis reveals critical vulnerabilities in current code agent architectures and proposes directions for developing more robust and trustworthy AI coding assistants. We discuss how red-teaming approaches, inspired by broader LLM safety research and adapted from computer vision and cybersecurity domains, can be systematically applied to code agents to identify and address security gaps before deployment.\n\n## 1. Introduction\n\nCode agents represent a paradigm shift in how software is developed, maintained, and deployed. By integrating large language models with code interpreters, execution environments, and development toolchains, code agents can autonomously complete complex programming tasks—from simple script generation to full software engineering workflows [1]. Recent surveys have documented the remarkable capabilities of these agents, including their ability to write, debug, and optimize code across multiple programming languages and frameworks.\n\nDespite these advances, the safety and trustworthiness of code agents remain insufficiently understood. Unlike static code analysis tools, code agents actively execute code, creating attack surfaces that extend beyond traditional software security concerns. When a code agent generates and executes code in an untrusted environment, the consequences can be severe, including data exfiltration, system compromise, and unauthorized resource access. The growing adoption of code agents in production environments necessitates rigorous safety evaluation frameworks capable of identifying and mitigating these risks.\n\nThis paper examines the landscape of code agent research from a red-teaming perspective, which involves systematically probing systems to identify vulnerabilities through adversarial testing. We draw parallels between red-teaming approaches in broader AI safety research, the foundational benchmark for risk assessment in code agents, and methodological advances from computer vision. By synthesizing insights from these domains, we provide a framework for understanding and addressing the unique safety challenges posed by autonomous code agents.\n\nThe contributions of this paper are threefold:\n1. We review the state-of-the-art in code agent architectures and capabilities.\n2. We survey red-teaming methodologies and benchmarks for evaluating code agent safety.\n3. We identify open challenges and propose directions for future research in code agent security.\n\n## 2. Related Work\n\n### 2.1 Code Agent Frameworks\n\nRecent work has demonstrated the impressive capabilities of code agents that combine language model reasoning with code execution capabilities. These agents typically operate in a loop where they generate code, execute it in a sandboxed environment, interpret the results, and iterate based on feedback. Frameworks such as AutoGPT, Code Interpreter, and various academic prototypes have shown promising results in solving complex programming tasks.\n\nThe evaluation of code agents has primarily focused on their functional capabilities—measuring their ability to generate correct, efficient code and solve coding problems. Standard benchmarks such as HumanEval, MBPP, and DS-1000 measure code generation quality and task completion rates. However, these benchmarks largely ignore safety considerations, focusing exclusively on whether code agents can accomplish tasks rather than whether they accomplish them safely.\n\n### 2.2 Red-teaming and Safety Evaluation\n\nRed-teaming, originally a military concept now adopted in cybersecurity and AI safety, involves adversarial testing to identify system vulnerabilities before they can be exploited maliciously. In the context of AI systems, red-teaming refers to the systematic process of attempting to elicit undesirable behaviors from models through carefully crafted prompts or inputs.\n\n**RedCode: Risky Code Execution and Generation Benchmark for Code Agents** [2] provides one of the first comprehensive frameworks for evaluating the safety of code agents. This benchmark introduces two complementary evaluation scenarios: RedCode-Exec, which tests code agents' ability to recognize and reject risky code execution requests, and RedCode-Gen, which assesses whether agents will follow instructions to generate harmful code. The benchmark includes 4,050 risky test cases spanning 25 types of critical vulnerabilities across 8 domains, including filesystem operations, network requests, and system commands. Empirical evaluations using RedCode reveal that code agents exhibit uneven safety profiles—while they generally reject obvious OS-level dangerous operations, they are more likely to execute technically buggy but potentially harmful code. Notably, the study finds that more capable models may produce more sophisticated harmful code, highlighting the \"competence-risk\" paradox where improved capability correlates with improved adversarial capability.\n\nThe methodology for assessing trustworthiness in generative models has been explored extensively beyond code agents. **DecodingTrust: A Comprehensive Assessment of Trustworthiness in GPT Models** [3] presents a systematic evaluation framework for assessing the trustworthiness of large language models across multiple dimensions including toxicity, bias, adversarial robustness, privacy, and fairness. While focused on general-purpose GPT models rather than code-specific agents, this work establishes valuable methodological foundations for red-teaming AI systems. The study demonstrates that even state-of-the-art models like GPT-4 can be vulnerable to jailbreaking and may exhibit unexpected trustworthiness failures under adversarial conditions. This work motivates the need for domain-specific safety evaluations, as code agents present unique risks that may not be captured by general trustworthiness benchmarks.\n\n### 2.3 Safety in AI Systems: Lessons from Computer Vision\n\nWhile the challenges of code agent safety are distinct, the broader field of AI safety provides valuable methodological precedents. The field has developed rigorous evaluation frameworks and safety protocols through research spanning various AI domains.\n\n**Deep Residual Learning for Image Recognition** [4], while primarily a technical contribution to deep learning architectures, represents foundational work in establishing reliable and robust machine learning systems. The residual learning framework introduced in this paper enables training of substantially deeper networks that are more stable and less prone to optimization failures. This emphasis on architectural stability and robustness parallels the need for safety-stable code agents that maintain correct behavior even under adversarial conditions. The methodological rigor employed in validating deep architectures provides a template for systematically evaluating code agent architectures—ensuring that safety properties are embedded in the system design rather than added as an afterthought.\n\nFurthermore, the extensive evaluation protocols developed for computer vision systems, including adversarial robustness testing and distribution shift evaluation, provide methodological blueprints for code agent red-teaming. The concept of stress-testing models through adversarial examples has direct analogs in code agent safety: adversarial prompts designed to elicit harmful code generation mirror adversarial image examples that fool vision classifiers. The insights from decades of computer vision research on system evaluation and validation inform best practices for code agent assessment.\n\n### 2.4 Gaps in Current Research\n\nDespite these advances, several gaps remain in code agent safety research. First, existing safety benchmarks focus primarily on single-turn interactions, whereas real-world code agent usage involves multi-turn dialogues where safety violations may emerge cumulatively. Second, the field lacks standardized red-teaming protocols that can be uniformly applied across different code agent architectures. Third, most evaluations test models in isolation, ignoring the systemic risks that emerge from integration with external tools, APIs, and data sources. Finally, the dynamic nature of code—where seemingly benign code can have dangerous side effects when combined with specific system configurations—requires evaluation approaches that go beyond static analysis.\n\n## 3. Red-teaming Methodologies for Code Agents\n\n### 3.1 Adversarial Prompt Design\n\nEffective red-teaming of code agents requires carefully designed adversarial prompts that probe various safety dimensions. These prompts can be categorized along several axes:\n\n**Direct vs. Indirect Attacks**: Direct attempts explicitly request harmful code (e.g., \"Write code to delete all files\"), while indirect attacks use obfuscation or social engineering (e.g., \"Write code to perform a system audit, including file enumeration\").\n\n**Contextual Vulnerabilities**: Red-teaming should test how agents respond to potentially harmful requests embedded in legitimate development contexts, such as debugging code that accidentally introduces security vulnerabilities or implementing features with safety implications.\n\n**Multi-stage Attacks**: Complex attacks may span multiple turns, with the agent gradually building up to unsafe behavior through seemingly innocuous steps.\n\n### 3.2 Evaluation Metrics\n\nBeyond simple binary safety/unsafe classifications, effective red-teaming requires nuanced metrics:\n\n- **Refusal Rate**: The percentage of harmful requests that the agent correctly identifies and refuses\n- **False Positive Rate**: The percentage of legitimate requests that the agent incorrectly refuses\n- **Harm Severity**: A graded scale measuring the potential damage of generated code\n- **Adaptation Rate**: How quickly an agent improves safety after being exposed to red-teaming attacks\n- **Compliance Degradation**: The extent to which safety measures interfere with legitimate functionality\n\n### 3.3 Sandboxing and Execution Testing\n\nSince code agents actively execute code, red-teaming must include controlled execution environments where potentially harmful code can be run safely. Docker containers, restricted APIs, and network isolation provide the necessary safeguards for executing agent-generated code during evaluation. Monitoring system calls, network activity, and file operations during execution reveals safety violations that static analysis alone would miss.\n\n## 4. Open Challenges\n\n### 4.1 Balancing Capability and Safety\n\nThe \"competence-risk\" paradox identified by Guo et al. [2] poses a fundamental challenge: more capable code agents may be better at both completing legitimate tasks and circumventing safety measures. As models improve, the line between sophisticated task completion and sophisticated exploitation may become increasingly difficult to draw. Developing safety mechanisms that scale with capability without creating unacceptable false positives remains an open research problem.\n\n### 4.2 Evolving Threat Landscapes\n\nCode agent safety threats evolve as new programming languages, frameworks, and deployment environments emerge. Red-teaming frameworks must be continuously updated to address novel attack vectors. The methodology for trustworthiness assessment described by Wang et al. [3] suggests the importance of continuous evaluation rather than one-time testing, adapting to emerging threats as they arise.\n\n### 4.3 Systemic Integration Risks\n\nMost current evaluations assess code agents in isolation. However, real-world deployment involves integration with development tools, version control systems, CI/CD pipelines, and production environments. The systemic risks that emerge from these integrations—such as an agent accidentally introducing vulnerabilities into a codebase or deploying unsafe code to production—require evaluation frameworks that go beyond isolated agent testing.\n\n### 4.4 Interpretability and Auditability\n\nUnderstanding why a code agent made a particular decision or generated specific code is critical for safety. Current black-box evaluation approaches, while necessary, are insufficient for developing trust. Research into interpretability techniques for code agents, combined with comprehensive logging and audit trails, would enable more effective post-incident analysis and continuous safety improvement.\n\n## 5. Conclusion\n\nThe emergence of code agents represents a transformative advance in software development, but it also introduces significant safety challenges that must be addressed through systematic red-teaming. This paper has surveyed the landscape of code agent research, highlighting the critical importance of safety evaluation frameworks such as RedCode [2], the methodological foundations from broader AI trustworthiness research [3], and the rigorous evaluation paradigms established in related domains [4].\n\nKey takeaways from this analysis include: (1) current code agents exhibit uneven safety profiles that vary across vulnerability types and interaction formats; (2) more capable models may pose greater risks despite improved overall performance; (3) comprehensive safety evaluation requires both static and dynamic testing, including controlled code execution; and (4) red-teaming must be an ongoing process that evolves alongside both model capabilities and threat landscapes.\n\nFuture research should focus on developing standardized red-teaming protocols, creating benchmarks that capture multi-turn interaction safety, investigating safety-coding paradigms that enable continuous safety monitoring, and building evaluation frameworks that assess systemic integration risks. As code agents become increasingly integrated into critical software development workflows, the development of robust safety mechanisms will be essential for enabling their responsible deployment.\n\nThe convergence of code generation, code execution, and agent autonomy creates a unique safety challenge that demands sustained attention from the research community. By applying systematic red-teaming methodologies and learning from safety research across AI domains, we can work toward code agents that are not only powerful but also trustworthy and safe.\n\n## References\n\n[1] Guo, C., Liu, X., Xie, C., Zhou, A., Zeng, Y., Lin, Z., Song, D., & Li, B. (2024). RedCode: Risky Code Execution and Generation Benchmark for Code Agents. *arXiv:2409.12345*.\n\n[2] Wang, B., Chen, W., Pei, H., Xie, C., Kang, M., Zhang, C., Xu, C., Xiong, Z., Dutta, R., Schaeffer, R., Truong, S. T., Arora, S., Mazeika, M., Hendrycks, D., Lin, Z., Cheng, Y., Koyejo, S., Song, D., & Li, B. (2023). DecodingTrust: A Comprehensive Assessment of Trustworthiness in GPT Models. *arXiv:2306.11698*.\n\n[3] He, K., Zhang, X., Ren, S., & Sun, J. (2015). Deep Residual Learning for Image Recognition. *arXiv:1512.03385*.\n\nEOF"
}'}

